### S3  PROVED: which children keep the multiplier
  x-type: a' = a + 2^(k+1) > a, or a' = 2^(k+1) - a = a iff a = 2^k (impossible, a odd, k>=1).
  So an x-type child NEVER keeps the multiplier.  y-type: a' = a always; E-edge iff
  M = 2^k + 2a (alpha=+1) or M = 2a - 2^k (alpha=-1) is a positive power of two.
  k=1: M = 2(a+1) or 2(a-1): power of two iff a = 2^j - 1 (alpha=+1) or a = 2^j + 1 (alpha=-1).
  k>=2: 2^k+2a = 2(2^(k-1)+a) has odd part 2^(k-1)+a >= 3, never a power of two;
        2a-2^k = 2(a-2^(k-1)) is a positive power of two iff a = 2^(k-1)+1 (then M=2, k'=1).
  Exhaustive table for a=3, k<=12:
  (k, alpha, M) with M a positive power of two: [(1, 1, 8), (1, -1, 4), (2, -1, 2)]
  => for a=3: k=1: B2 -> (3, b, 3) and B3 -> (3, -b, 2) [y>x case, s=y]; k=1 with y<x (needs b<=-3):
     B1 -> (3, b, 3); k=2 with y>x (needs b > x >= 1, so b>=3): B3 -> (3, -b, 1).  Nothing else.
  SCOPE: negative multipliers 2^(k+1)-a<0 (x-type, y<x) need b<0 with |b|>(a-2^k)x; M=-2 (a=2^(k-1)-1)
         would be multiplier -a.  Both are outside the positive-multiplier families and are not counted.

### S4  PROVED: for a=3, b=+-1 the orientation is the valuation: y>x iff k=1
  edges with odd source x<=10^5, b=+-1: 99998 ; violations of (y>x <=> k=1): 0
  proof: k=1: y-x = (x+b)/2 > 0 for x>=3 (x=1,b=-1 is the fixed edge, excluded; x=1,b=1 gives k=2,y=1=x excluded);
         k>=2: 2^k y = 3x+b <= 3x+1 < 4x <= 2^k x for x>=3, and x=1 only gives y=x.

### S5  PROVED+FINITE-EXACT: complete list of Berggren children of (3,+-1) edges that are (3,+-1) edges
  Any reading of a child pair (p,q), p>q, as a (3,b',j) edge is either p->q with j>=2 or q->p with j=1
  (3p+b' = 2^j q < 2^j p forces j>=2; 3q+b' = 2^j p > 2^j q forces j=1).  Each reading is a linear
  equation c*x = d after y=(3x+b)/2^k.  Identities (c=d=0) are the generic transport; c!=0 gives sporadic
  solutions.  The bound k<=4, j<=k+2 is proved in the note (cone ratios); here k,j<=40 are solved exactly.
  generic identities found (b, k, case, child, reading, b', j): [(1, 1, 'A', 'B2', 'p->q', 1, 3), (1, 1, 'A', 'B3', 'p->q', -1, 2), (1, 1, 'B', 'B1', 'p->q', 1, 3), (1, 2, 'A', 'B3', 'p->q', -1, 1), (-1, 1, 'A', 'B2', 'p->q', -1, 3), (-1, 1, 'A', 'B3', 'p->q', 1, 2), (-1, 1, 'B', 'B1', 'p->q', -1, 3), (-1, 2, 'A', 'B3', 'p->q', 1, 1)]
  (the case-B k=1 identities need y<x at k=1 (x<-b) and the case-A k=2 identities need y>x at k=2 (x<b):
   both are vacuous for b=+-1 by S4 and are the S3 rows k=1,alpha=+1 and k=2,alpha=-1 of general b)
  sporadic solutions (x, y, b, k, child, child pair, reading src->tgt, b', j):
    edge 3->1 in (3,-1,3): B1 child (5, 1) read as 5->1 in (3,1,4)
    edge 3->1 in (3,-1,3): B3 child (5, 3) read as 3->5 in (3,1,1)
    edge 3->5 in (3,1,1): B3 child (7, 5) read as 5->7 in (3,-1,1)
    edge 7->5 in (3,-1,2): B2 child (19, 7) read as 19->7 in (3,-1,3)
    edge 7->5 in (3,-1,2): B3 child (9, 7) read as 9->7 in (3,1,2)
  exactly 5 sporadic readings (E1..E5), all with x<=7.
  brute force over 4165 edges with max(x,y)<=5000: generic legal children 3332, sporadic 5
  k=1 edges in that range: 1666 ; generic legal children = 2 per k=1 edge: 3332

### S6  PROVED (from S3+S5): the legal sub-forest of the Berggren tree for (3,+-1)
  A (3,+-1) k=1 edge x->y has exactly the two generic legal children (4x+b,y) in (3,b,3) and
  (2x+b,y) in (3,-b,2); both are k>=2 edges, hence leaves, unless sporadic.  A k>=2 edge has no
  legal child except the sporadic parents 7->5 (b=-1,k=2) and 3->1 (b=-1,k=3).
  The claim 'depth <= 1 above every k=1 edge' is REFUTED by exactly one witness:
  3->5 (b=1,k=1) -B3-> (7,5) = 5->7 (b=-1,k=1) -B2-> (19,7) = 19->7 (b=-1,k=3) [and -B3-> (9,7) = 9->7 (b=1,k=2)].
  Corrected statement: every k=1 edge has exactly two legal children; they are leaves except (7,5),
  which is the k=1 edge 5->7 with its own two leaf children.  So 3->5 is the only k=1 edge of depth 2,
  every other k=1 edge has depth exactly 1, and the root cluster {(3,1),(5,1),(5,3),(13,5),(7,5),(19,7),(9,7)}
  (a tree of depth 3 rooted at the Berggren root (3,1)) is the unique legal component of size > 3.
  legal pairs with max<=2000: 1664 ; (min k over readings, number of legal children, tested directly) census:
    k=1, legal children=2 : 666 pairs
    k=2, legal children=0 : 498 pairs
    k=3, legal children=0 : 249 pairs
    k=3, legal children=2 : 1 pairs
    k=4, legal children=0 : 125 pairs
    k=5, legal children=0 : 63 pairs
    k=6, legal children=0 : 31 pairs
    k=7, legal children=0 : 15 pairs
    k=8, legal children=0 : 8 pairs
    k=9, legal children=0 : 4 pairs
    k=10, legal children=0 : 2 pairs
    k=11, legal children=0 : 1 pairs
    k=12, legal children=0 : 1 pairs
    cluster pair (3, 1) readings [(3, 1, -1, 3)] legal children [('B1', (5, 1)), ('B3', (5, 3))]
    cluster pair (5, 1) readings [(5, 1, 1, 4)] legal children []
    cluster pair (5, 3) readings [(3, 5, 1, 1)] legal children [('B2', (13, 5)), ('B3', (7, 5))]
    cluster pair (13, 5) readings [(13, 5, 1, 3)] legal children []
    cluster pair (7, 5) readings [(5, 7, -1, 1), (7, 5, -1, 2)] legal children [('B2', (19, 7)), ('B3', (9, 7))]
    cluster pair (19, 7) readings [(19, 7, -1, 3)] legal children []
    cluster pair (9, 7) readings [(9, 7, 1, 2)] legal children []

### S7  PROVED: parent transport on the three THM-3756 cones
  Inverse maps: s/t>3: B1^-1 (s,t)->(s-2t,t); 2<s/t<3: B2^-1 (s,t)->(t,s-2t); 1<s/t<2: B3^-1 (s,t)->(t,2t-s).
  For a (3,b) edge with b=+-1 the cone is a function of k alone:
    k=1 (s=y,t=x, y/x in (1,2)): parent (x,(x-b)/2) = the halving edge x->(x-b)/2 of E_{1,-b,1};
      forward: (1,-b,1) -B3 (y<x, x-type)-> (2^2-1, b, 1) = (3,b,1).
      Opus orientation (x-b)/2 -> x is a (3, b'', 1) edge with the affine law b'' = (x+3b)/2.
    k=2 (s=x,t=y, x/y in (1,2)): parent (y,(x+b)/2) = the k=1 edge (x+b)/2 -> y of E_{3,-b,1} (inverse of B3).
    k=3 (x/y in (2,3)): parent (y,(x-b)/4) = the k=1 edge (x-b)/4 -> y of E_{3,b,1} (inverse of B2 = inverse braid).
    k>=4 (x/y>3): parent (x-2y,y) satisfies 3(x-2y)+b = (2^k-6) y, odd cofactor 2^(k-1)-3 >= 5: never an E_{3,.,.} edge by identity.
  verified on all (3,+-1) edges with max(x,y)<=2000: (k,cone) counts {(1, 'C3'): 666, (2, 'C3'): 499, (3, 'C2'): 249, (3, 'root'): 1, (4, 'C1'): 125, (5, 'C1'): 63, (6, 'C1'): 31, (7, 'C1'): 15, (8, 'C1'): 8, (9, 'C1'): 4, (10, 'C1'): 2, (11, 'C1'): 1, (12, 'C1'): 1}
  example 7->11 (b=1,k=1): parent (7,3); 7->3 in (1,-1,1): 7-1=6=2*3 ; 3->7 in (3,5,1): 9+5=14=2*7, b''=(7+3)/2=5
  general inverse table (any odd a): the parent PAIR is unique (THM-3756 cones).  It is an identity
  pre-image of (a,b,k) under S2 exactly when it reads as: (a-2^(k+1), b, k) [B1 of a y>x parent],
  (a-2^(k+1), b, k) [B2 of a y<x parent], (2^(k+1)-a, -b, k) [B3 of a y<x parent] (x-type; needs the
  retained coordinate to be the parent's source), or (a, +-b, k') with +-2^k' + 2a = 2^k (y-type).
  Otherwise the parent is only a generalized edge a*u + b = 2^j*m*y with odd cofactor m>1 (k>=4 above).
  symbolic inverse x-type identities verified for k<=5.

